    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f4bdd6a9-2425-5463-85cb-734e8dc3548a",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=27 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=18.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(18)=\\frac{9}{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}-(-18)=\\frac{45}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{45}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=18$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{45}{2})-(18)=27\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=27-(\\frac{45}{2})-(-18)=\\frac{45}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{45}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{45}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=18.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨\\frac{45}{2},\\frac{45}{2},18⟩.$",
